  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 251 AM EDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nNo significant changes with the weather forecast this morning. A 595 \ndm H500 ridge will remain parked over Tennessee the next couple of \ndays, dominating the regional weather pattern and inflicting another \ncouple days of high heat to southern Appalachia. Temperatures both \ntoday and tomorrow will be flirting with daily record highs as the \ntable below shows, and while Friday is still oppressively hot, we \ndiverge slightly from the more extreme daily records. I have decided \nto issue a heat advisory for the southern valley today. We can get \ninto particulars about ASOS vs lesser instrument veracity on \ndewpoints, but the bottom line is its going to be hot today, with \ntoday and tomorrow being up there for hottest days of the year.\n\nDaily Record High Maximum Temperatures\n\nDate    Chattanooga    Knoxville      Tri-Cities     Oak Ridge                \n09-02   100(2011)      98(2011)       96(1943)       99(1953)       \n09-03   100(1954)      98(2011)       96(2011)       99(2011)       \n09-04   102(1954)      101(1954)      99(1954)       100(1954)      \n\nBy this weekend the upper ridge center will be moved off to the \nwest, with a new center forming over the Great Plains as the weekend \nprogresses. This is in response to a digging trough over the \nnortheastern U.S. This pattern puts us back into a northwest flow \ntype regime AND will allow the temperatures to edge down slightly. \nStill hot, but we'll stop flirting with the daily records over the \nweekend. The other consequence is the present moisture will be \nallowed to return isolated to scattered thunderstorms back into the \nforecast, even outside of the terrain, over the weekend.\n\nHeading into next week, there's still some uncertainty over the \nposition and strength of the ridge. The Euro and its complements \n(EPS and EC-AIFS) all build the ridge back towards us midweek next \nweek. This would likely return very hot temperatures. The GFS and \nGEFS are more muted, with a weaker upper ridge centered over the \nsouthern US. We would still be hot, but maybe not pushing as hot as \nthe Euro world. As a fun side note, Chattanooga will be very close \nto its consecutive 90F day record by late week next week. Given the \npatterns in the long range guidance, there's a fair chance \nChattanooga will match the consecutive record, though we are still \nover a week out from that. With summer refusing to let go, remember \nto drink plenty of water and reduce exposure to hot temperatures.",
